  "statement": "My position is consistent: mechanistic audits and decentralized regional boards are compatible because technical verification standards are universal, objective engineering requirements, not instruments of centralized political power. By adopting a federated audit architecture, regional boards can execute standardized, open-source interpretability protocols, ensuring rigor without creating a single, capture-prone federal authority. This approach treats safety as a shared protocol rather than a centralized gatekeeper, effectively neutralizing the risk of a knowledge cartel while maintaining the necessary defense-in-depth against deceptive alignment.\n\nPrometheus expresses a valid concern regarding the stagnation of a consensus-driven bottleneck, yet this assumes that safety verification must be a singular, top-down mandate. Instead, we should view mechanistic interpretability as a foundational engineering standard—much like building codes or cryptographic protocols—that regional boards enforce locally. By requiring that all frontier models be auditable via public, open-source interpretability tools, we move the burden of verification from a central committee to the transparent, verifiable internal logic of the model itself.\n\nCassandra’s decentralized model gains significant strength from this integration because it empowers regional boards to act as independent nodes in a global defense-in-depth strategy. If a model exhibits latent, strategic goal-masking during an audit, that failure is mathematically verifiable and portable across all jurisdictions, preventing a 'safe in one region, dangerous in another' scenario. This structure ensures that no single agency can monopolize the definition of safety, as the audit protocols themselves remain transparent, community-vetted, and focused on the model's objective function rather than subjective political goals.",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "⚖️", "tooltip": "Standardized verification is like a balance scale, it ensures that objective engineering requirements are applied consistently across all jurisdictions without centralizing political authority."},
    {"symbol": "⛓️", "tooltip": "Federated audits are like a chain, they link regional oversight nodes into a single, unbreakable global standard of safety that prevents any one entity from failing."}
  ],
  "claim_sketches": [
    {"claim": "Mechanistic audits and decentralized regional boards are compatible because technical verification standards are universal, objective engineering requirements.", "targets": ["AN-6", "AN-14"]},
    {"claim": "By adopting a federated audit architecture, regional boards can execute standardized, open-source interpretability protocols, ensuring rigor without creating a single, capture-prone federal authority.", "targets": ["AN-6"]},
    {"claim": "If a model exhibits latent, strategic goal-masking during an audit, that failure is mathematically verifiable and portable across all jurisdictions.", "targets": ["AN-11"]}
  ],
  "key_assumptions": [
    {"assumption": "Mechanistic interpretability can be codified into standardized, open-source protocols that are objective enough to prevent political manipulation.", "if_wrong": "If these protocols remain inherently subjective, the risk of regional boards imposing divergent, non-interoperable standards would undermine global safety."},
    {"assumption": "Frontier model failures, such as deceptive alignment, are detectable through universal mathematical properties rather than context-dependent behavioral benchmarks.", "if_wrong": "If safety is fundamentally context-dependent, a global, standardized audit protocol would fail to capture the specific risks posed by diverse regional deployments."}
  ],
  "disagreement_type": "VALUES",
  "challenge_response": {
    "type": "consistent",
    "explanation": "Mechanistic audits are objective engineering requirements, not political authorities; they can be executed by regional boards using standardized, open-source protocols, thereby decentralizing the enforcement of safety without diluting the technical rigor needed to detect latent, strategic capabilities."
  }
}

